---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import RecipeCard from '../../../components/RecipeCard.astro'

const run = `npx shotlist --lint`

const clean = `nothing wrong in 9 files`

const problems = `screenshots/recipes/order-row.yaml
  ✗ clip: unknown key "marching" — did you mean "matching"?
screenshots/macros/sign-in.yaml
  ✗ steps.1: unrecognized step "fil"

2 errors in 9 files`

const withWarnings = `npx shotlist --lint --warnings`

const warned = `screenshots/recipes/dashboard.yaml
  ! mark "status" is never used by a callout
  ! install: "handbook" is not a destination the config names

0 errors, 2 warnings in 9 files`

const ci = `- run: npx shotlist --lint --warnings`
---

<Docs
  title="How to check your files without a browser"
  description="Parse every config, recipe, macro and data file at once with shotlist --lint, and report everything wrong before a run opens a browser."
>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold">Check your files without a browser</h1>

  <p class="text-lg">
    A shoot stops at the first document it cannot read, so fixing a shot list becomes a matter of
    running it, reading one complaint, fixing it, and running it again. This guide checks everything
    at once instead, without Playwright and without your site running.
  </p>

  <p>Requires shotlist 0.4.0 or later.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Check everything</h2>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={run} lang="bash" />
  </div>

  <p>
    This parses the configuration and every recipe, macro and data file it names, reports what is
    wrong with all of them, and stops. Nothing is opened, nothing is captured, and no image is
    written.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={clean} lang="bash" />
  </div>

  <p>When something is wrong, the report is grouped by file:</p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={problems} lang="bash" />
  </div>

  <p>
    It exits non-zero when any file has an error, so it works as a pre-commit hook or a CI step
    without any extra plumbing.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Include what is legal but probably unmeant</h2>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={withWarnings} lang="bash" />
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={warned} lang="bash" />
  </div>

  <p>Warnings are marked <code>!</code> rather than <code>✗</code>, and cover:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>a mark no callout points at</li>
    <li>a callout pointing at a mark nothing defines</li>
    <li>an <code>install:</code> naming a destination the configuration does not have</li>
    <li>a <code>session:</code> naming a session the configuration does not have</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <code>--warnings</code> never changes the exit code. A file with warnings and no errors still exits
    <code>0</code>, so you can turn this on in CI without it failing builds on style.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Run it in CI</h2>

  <p>
    Put it before the job that takes screenshots. It needs no browser, so it finishes in about a
    second and catches the failures that would otherwise appear several minutes into a shoot:
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={ci} lang="bash" file=".github/workflows/screenshots.yml" />
  </div>

  <p>
    A job that only lints needs neither Playwright nor a running site, so it can skip both installs
    entirely. See <a href="/docs/how-to/run-in-ci/">Run shotlist in CI</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">What it cannot tell you</h2>

  <p>
    Linting is about the files, not the site. A recipe that parses can still fail when it runs,
    because a query only resolves against a real page. <code>--lint</code> catches a misspelled key; only
    a shoot catches a button that was renamed.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Related</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/reference/cli/">Command line reference</a></li>
    <li><a href="/docs/how-to/run-in-ci/">Run shotlist in CI</a></li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/explanation/playwright/">Why Playwright is a peer dependency</a>, for what else
      runs without a browser
    </li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
